1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,817
Push electrons along one direction,
and the current bends, flowing off to the side.

2
00:00:05,057 --> 00:00:07,024
And there is no magnetic field anywhere.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,336
This is the nonlinear Hall effect.

4
00:00:09,576 --> 00:00:12,167
At weak fields it is a second-order effect

5
00:00:12,187 --> 00:00:16,208
the transverse current grows as
the square of the electric field.

6
00:00:16,783 --> 00:00:18,493
But what happens when the field is strong?

7
00:00:18,733 --> 00:00:23,054
The paper we look at today studies
exactly this: the nonlinear Hall

8
00:00:23,074 --> 00:00:27,135
effect in nonequilibrium steady
states driven by strong fields.

9
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:31,741
First, recall the ordinary Hall effect.

10
00:00:31,981 --> 00:00:36,238
An electron moving through a magnetic
field feels the Lorentz force

11
00:00:36,258 --> 00:00:39,941
gets deflected sideways,
and a transverse current appears.

12
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:45,425
But in some materials, a transverse current
appears even without a magnetic field.

13
00:00:45,665 --> 00:00:49,700
The reason is hidden in momentum space:
the wavefunctions of Bloch

14
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:53,633
electrons carry a geometric property
called the Berry curvature.

15
00:00:53,873 --> 00:00:57,331
It acts like an effective
magnetic field in momentum space.

16
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:01,861
In an electric field,
an electron picks up an anomalous velocity.

17
00:01:02,101 --> 00:01:04,043
It points perpendicular to the field

18
00:01:04,063 --> 00:01:06,660
with a size proportional
to the Berry curvature.

19
00:01:07,217 --> 00:01:09,510
Add up the anomalous velocities of all the

20
00:01:09,530 --> 00:01:12,043
occupied states,
and you get the Hall current.

21
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,632
But if the material has
time-reversal symmetry

22
00:01:15,652 --> 00:01:20,807
the Berry curvature at k and at minus k
is equal in size and opposite in sign.

23
00:01:21,047 --> 00:01:24,282
Positive and negative cancel,
the integral vanishes

24
00:01:24,302 --> 00:01:26,516
and the linear Hall effect is gone.

25
00:01:27,883 --> 00:01:29,520
The way out appears at second order.

26
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:34,521
The electric field pushes the whole Fermi sea,
shifting it through momentum space.

27
00:01:35,083 --> 00:01:39,518
After the shift, the positive and
negative Berry curvature covered by

28
00:01:39,538 --> 00:01:43,972
the Fermi sea no longer balance,
so a net anomalous velocity appears.

29
00:01:44,212 --> 00:01:48,968
The shift is proportional to the field,
and the anomalous velocity is proportional

30
00:01:48,988 --> 00:01:53,277
to the field again, so the transverse
current scales as the field squared.

31
00:01:53,833 --> 00:01:56,276
This is the Berry curvature dipole picture.

32
00:01:56,516 --> 00:01:59,860
It successfully explains the
second-order Hall signals

33
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:03,286
seen in materials such as
bilayer tungsten ditelluride.

34
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:08,367
However, in a Weyl Kondo semimetal,
experiments found a giant Hall response

35
00:02:08,387 --> 00:02:12,782
that clearly departs from the square
law predicted by the dipole picture.

36
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:16,439
The issue is that the dipole
picture is fundamentally

37
00:02:16,459 --> 00:02:18,961
perturbative: it assumes the shift is tiny.

38
00:02:19,201 --> 00:02:23,147
Once the field is strong enough to
push the Fermi sea toward the hot

39
00:02:23,167 --> 00:02:27,229
spots where the Berry curvature is
concentrated, or to drive electrons

40
00:02:27,249 --> 00:02:30,845
between the two bands,
the perturbative expansion breaks down.

41
00:02:31,085 --> 00:02:32,744
We need a nonperturbative theory.

42
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:38,201
The authors study this with the simplest
possible two-band lattice model.

43
00:02:38,767 --> 00:02:42,280
The three Pauli matrices act on
an orbital degree of freedom.

44
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:45,576
With D x equal to one
half and D y equal to one

45
00:02:45,596 --> 00:02:49,700
the two bands touch at two points,
forming two Weyl-like nodes.

46
00:02:50,283 --> 00:02:53,877
The Berry curvature is strongly
concentrated near these two nodes

47
00:02:53,897 --> 00:02:56,046
with opposite signs at the two of them.

48
00:02:56,286 --> 00:02:58,799
We call these regions
Berry curvature hot spots.

49
00:02:59,367 --> 00:03:03,334
The model keeps time-reversal symmetry
but breaks inversion symmetry.

50
00:03:03,574 --> 00:03:07,229
So it has no linear Hall effect,
but it can have a nonlinear one.

51
00:03:07,783 --> 00:03:10,423
At a chemical potential of zero point three

52
00:03:10,443 --> 00:03:14,445
the Fermi surface consists of two
small pockets around the nodes.

53
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,984
So how do we describe a
lattice driven by a constant

54
00:03:19,004 --> 00:03:21,879
electric field,
without expanding in the field?

55
00:03:23,233 --> 00:03:27,984
Here is the first difficulty:
a DC field keeps accelerating the electrons.

56
00:03:28,224 --> 00:03:32,278
On a lattice, their momentum sweeps through
the Brillouin zone again and again.

57
00:03:32,518 --> 00:03:36,443
Without dissipation,
the work done by the field has nowhere to go

58
00:03:36,463 --> 00:03:39,417
and the system never settles
into a steady state.

59
00:03:40,683 --> 00:03:44,522
So the authors couple every lattice
site to a fermionic reservoir.

60
00:03:44,762 --> 00:03:48,454
The reservoir exchanges particles
and energy at a rate Gamma.

61
00:03:48,694 --> 00:03:53,285
Energy pumped in by the field balances
energy drained by the reservoirs

62
00:03:53,305 --> 00:03:56,727
and the system reaches a
nonequilibrium steady state.

63
00:03:57,983 --> 00:04:02,689
In this steady state, the electron
momentum drifts along the field at rate E

64
00:04:02,709 --> 00:04:05,799
and survives for a time of
roughly one over Gamma.

65
00:04:06,039 --> 00:04:09,504
So the occupation ends up
shifted by roughly E over Gamma.

66
00:04:11,633 --> 00:04:16,529
To handle all of this exactly,
the authors use nonequilibrium Green's functions.

67
00:04:16,769 --> 00:04:21,436
The field enters through the Moyal star
product in the Wigner representation

68
00:04:21,456 --> 00:04:25,938
and the natural momentum variable is
the gauge-covariant momentum, kappa.

69
00:04:26,178 --> 00:04:29,795
Nowhere does the calculation
expand in powers of the field.

70
00:04:30,950 --> 00:04:35,970
Once you have the steady-state density matrix,
the Hall current is the trace of the

71
00:04:35,990 --> 00:04:40,768
velocity operator times the density matrix,
integrated over the Brillouin zone.

72
00:04:42,133 --> 00:04:46,581
Compared with the semiclassical Boltzmann equation,
this approach keeps the

73
00:04:46,601 --> 00:04:50,930
quantum coherence between bands,
does not rely on a quasiparticle picture

74
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:55,696
and can include interactions and
electron-phonon coupling through self-energies.

75
00:04:57,050 --> 00:04:58,237
Let's look at the results.

76
00:04:58,477 --> 00:05:01,400
This is the transverse
current divided by the field

77
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:03,247
as a function of field strength.

78
00:05:03,487 --> 00:05:06,063
Each curve is a different chemical potential

79
00:05:06,083 --> 00:05:08,837
with the damping Gamma
fixed at zero point one.

80
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:14,029
At weak fields the curves are nearly
straight lines, meaning the transverse

81
00:05:14,049 --> 00:05:19,112
current goes as the field squared,
exactly as the Berry curvature dipole predicts.

82
00:05:19,667 --> 00:05:22,554
But near a field of about
zero point zero three

83
00:05:22,574 --> 00:05:26,142
the curves for larger chemical
potentials bend noticeably.

84
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:31,540
Further up, when the field becomes comparable
to the damping Gamma, a bump appears.

85
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:36,530
At even stronger fields, the Hall signal
can actually decrease as the field grows.

86
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:41,333
The small wiggles on the curves come
from Landau, Zener, Stueckelberg

87
00:05:41,353 --> 00:05:45,894
interference between the coherent
amplitudes of the upper and lower bands.

88
00:05:47,250 --> 00:05:48,426
Where does the bend come from?

89
00:05:48,666 --> 00:05:50,380
Let's go back to momentum space.

90
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:54,453
At a chemical potential of zero point three,
the edge of the left

91
00:05:54,473 --> 00:05:58,544
pocket sits a distance of zero point
three two two two from its node.

92
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:00,103
Now turn on the field.

93
00:06:00,343 --> 00:06:05,606
This is the steady-state occupation,
computed with nonequilibrium Green's functions.

94
00:06:05,846 --> 00:06:08,259
As the field grows, the whole occupation

95
00:06:08,279 --> 00:06:10,996
drifts to the right,
by roughly E over Gamma.

96
00:06:11,550 --> 00:06:14,525
When the drift equals that distance,
the edge of the

97
00:06:14,545 --> 00:06:17,578
Fermi surface runs into the
Berry curvature hot spot.

98
00:06:17,818 --> 00:06:20,865
From here on,
the dipole expansion no longer holds.

99
00:06:21,105 --> 00:06:24,466
So the critical field is roughly
Gamma times this distance.

100
00:06:25,033 --> 00:06:29,524
Check this with different values of Gamma:
for every curve, the bend lands

101
00:06:29,544 --> 00:06:34,157
right near the point where E over Gamma
equals zero point three two two two.

102
00:06:35,517 --> 00:06:38,725
But if the chemical potential
sits exactly at the nodes

103
00:06:38,745 --> 00:06:43,479
there is no Fermi surface at all,
so where does the transverse current come from?

104
00:06:44,034 --> 00:06:46,078
The answer is interband transitions.

105
00:06:46,318 --> 00:06:50,065
Near the cones, the field pushes
electrons from the lower band

106
00:06:50,085 --> 00:06:53,954
into the upper band,
leaving a hole behind at the same momentum.

107
00:06:54,194 --> 00:06:57,100
Drift and dissipation
drag these electron-hole

108
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:59,836
pairs away from the nodes, along the field.

109
00:07:00,076 --> 00:07:02,603
The stronger the field,
the farther they are dragged.

110
00:07:03,967 --> 00:07:06,772
In this regime,
can we still compute the Hall current

111
00:07:06,792 --> 00:07:09,597
from the Berry-curvature-weighted
occupation formula?

112
00:07:09,837 --> 00:07:11,672
The authors compared the two directly.

113
00:07:12,234 --> 00:07:14,713
At a chemical potential of zero point three

114
00:07:14,733 --> 00:07:17,388
and weak fields,
all three methods agree well.

115
00:07:17,628 --> 00:07:20,887
But once interband processes matter,
the Berry curvature

116
00:07:20,907 --> 00:07:23,816
formula departs clearly
from the full calculation.

117
00:07:24,384 --> 00:07:29,302
At zero chemical potential it is even starker:
a rigidly shifted equilibrium

118
00:07:29,322 --> 00:07:34,566
distribution gives exactly zero Hall current,
while the true current is not zero.

119
00:07:35,934 --> 00:07:37,215
There is one more subtlety.

120
00:07:37,455 --> 00:07:40,845
The constant relaxation time
approximation predicts a

121
00:07:40,865 --> 00:07:44,255
critical field twice as large
as the reservoir model.

122
00:07:44,495 --> 00:07:47,177
The hot-spot criterion itself is universal

123
00:07:47,197 --> 00:07:51,487
but the actual critical field depends
on how the system dissipates.

124
00:07:52,850 --> 00:07:56,067
The framework also gives
frequency-resolved information

125
00:07:56,087 --> 00:07:58,656
such as the effective distribution function.

126
00:07:58,896 --> 00:08:01,374
In equilibrium, it is simply a Fermi step.

127
00:08:01,933 --> 00:08:04,262
At weak fields, the step just gets smoother.

128
00:08:04,502 --> 00:08:07,055
At strong fields, the distribution develops

129
00:08:07,075 --> 00:08:09,508
clear wiggles and a left-right asymmetry.

130
00:08:09,748 --> 00:08:13,474
This marks the complete breakdown
of the perturbative regime

131
00:08:13,494 --> 00:08:17,656
as the system enters a strongly
driven nonequilibrium steady state.

132
00:08:19,017 --> 00:08:22,210
Finally, real materials
also have electron-phonon

133
00:08:22,230 --> 00:08:24,505
coupling and electron correlations.

134
00:08:24,745 --> 00:08:26,850
Within dynamical mean-field theory

135
00:08:26,870 --> 00:08:29,977
the authors add phonons
and a Hubbard interaction.

136
00:08:30,534 --> 00:08:34,812
The overall shape of the Hall
signal versus field does not change

137
00:08:34,832 --> 00:08:38,714
so the nonperturbative mechanisms
found earlier are robust.

138
00:08:38,954 --> 00:08:41,863
But the size of the signal
is substantially enhanced.

139
00:08:43,217 --> 00:08:45,857
The enhancement comes
mainly from the real part

140
00:08:45,877 --> 00:08:48,291
of the self-energy, not from extra damping.

141
00:08:48,531 --> 00:08:53,048
The real part renormalizes the bands,
and opens a small gap at the nodes.

142
00:08:54,767 --> 00:08:57,666
The gap is small,
yet it makes the Berry curvature

143
00:08:57,686 --> 00:09:00,060
hot spots stronger and more concentrated.

144
00:09:00,617 --> 00:09:05,559
In fact, simply replacing D y with an
effective value lets a noninteracting

145
00:09:05,579 --> 00:09:10,323
model reproduce the full electron-phonon
result to within a few percent.

146
00:09:11,683 --> 00:09:12,307
To sum up.

147
00:09:12,866 --> 00:09:15,707
First, the Berry curvature
dipole picture is only

148
00:09:15,727 --> 00:09:19,035
controlled at large enough
doping and weak enough fields.

149
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:24,677
Second, it breaks down when the driven
Fermi-surface edge reaches the Berry curvature

150
00:09:24,697 --> 00:09:29,354
hot spots near the nodes,
or when interband charge transfer becomes important.

151
00:09:29,594 --> 00:09:32,459
In those regimes,
the Berry-curvature-weighted

152
00:09:32,479 --> 00:09:35,468
occupation formula is no
longer reliable either.

153
00:09:36,033 --> 00:09:40,024
Third, the nonequilibrium
steady-state Green's function method

154
00:09:40,044 --> 00:09:44,100
computes the full Hall current
nonperturbatively, and naturally

155
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:48,304
includes dissipation, interactions,
and electron-phonon coupling.

156
00:09:48,544 --> 00:09:51,546
These do more than add damping,
because through band

157
00:09:51,566 --> 00:09:54,975
renormalization they reshape
the Berry curvature hot spots.

158
00:09:55,533 --> 00:09:58,263
This gives a quantitative
theoretical tool for

159
00:09:58,283 --> 00:10:01,371
strong-field transport and
spectroscopy experiments.

160
00:10:01,611 --> 00:10:02,472
Thanks for watching.
